        <body>
            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="COU01" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>John</forename>
                        <surname>Courtenay</surname>
                        <addName>Courtney</addName>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1738-08-22">
                        <placeName>Ireland</placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1816-03-24"/>
                    <occupation>politician</occupation>
                    <occupation>pamphleteer</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Godwin's contacts with him begin in
    1794 (confirmed by the 1796 list of contacts), in association with <persName key="JOH01">Johnson</persName> and <persName key="MAC01">Mackintosh</persName>. 
      There is also a K Courtenay, which may be
    Courtenay's son Kenneth who was given a chaplaincy sinecure as a result of his father's services
    in 1802-3. Courtenay is also present at <persName key="TOO01">Tooke's</persName> trial and in 1796 Godwin meets him at
   <persName key="LAU01"> Lauderdale's</persName> and <persName key="DEB01">Debrett's</persName>. The later entries are associated with Mackintosh, Tooke and <persName key="BUR02">Burdett</persName>.
    Courtenay dies in 1816. The two entries in 1821, write to Courtenay, have not been coded; nor
    have the six entries for K Courtenay, the four for P Courtenay, and the Courtney entries have
  been checked but seem not to refer to J Courtenay.</p>
                        <bibl type="other">
                            <title>DNB</title>
                        </bibl>
                    </note>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
